WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.080
Meet Escouade: the development environment built for your agents.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.580
Five projects. Twelve agents. Thirty terminals open… Seriously?

3
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:14.887
Escouade brings it all together in a single window.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.533
One project, one tab, one color: the whole interface follows. A green dot?

5
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:23.920
An agent is at work. The number? Modified files.

6
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:29.033
Drag tabs to sort them. Right-click: rename, recolor, close.

7
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:34.433
New project? Pick its folder: Escouade detects the git repository… or creates it.

8
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:39.053
A color, a first agent, and if you want, a git worktree per agent.

9
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.080
Agents? As many as you want, each with its own conversation.

10
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:50.207
Its card says it all: status, model, cost… and how warm its cache is. Hot,

11
00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:51.567
the next turn costs less.

12
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.380
Your first request? Haiku names it.

13
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.987
It’s done? Archive it: its conversation stays one click away.

14
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:01.093
Double-click to rename it.

15
00:01:01.173 --> 00:01:04.453
And Delete stops its process and removes its worktree.

16
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:10.807
This is a real chat: markdown, colored code, Claude’s thinking folded away,

17
00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:12.247
compact tool calls.

18
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:16.407
Unfold them: a change’s diff, a command’s output.

19
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.540
During the turn, you see what the agent does.

20
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.060
Its tokens and cost climb live.

21
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.887
A question? A permission? Answer in one click.

22
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.273
Write to it while it works: it factors your message in at the next step.

23
00:01:30.327 --> 00:01:32.060
And Esc stops it cold.

24
00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:38.807
Model, effort, mode: change them whenever you want. Fable, Opus, Sonnet or Haiku,

25
00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:40.487
in the version Claude Code uses.

26
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:45.787
Drop in an image, a PDF, a file. Mention a file with @,

27
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:47.787
run a command with /.

28
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:54.113
Done? A card sums up the task: time, cost, files. One click to review them,

29
00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:55.873
another to ask for the commit.

30
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.720
An agent is waiting for you, or finished? Its tab and card blink,

31
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.800
and a chime rings, which you can mute.

32
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.360
Escouade in the background? A notification arrives,

33
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.120
the taskbar flashes.

34
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:11.613
Ctrl+J: straight to the next agent waiting for you.

35
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:14.573
You close the window? Nothing stops:

36
00:02:14.653 --> 00:02:17.933
Escouade slips into the notification area. And on restart,

37
00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:19.693
every agent picks up its session.

38
00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:23.273
Split view: the conversation on the left,

39
00:02:23.313 --> 00:02:27.353
the modified files and their diff on the right, live while the agent writes.

40
00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:32.947
This agent or the whole project. Side by side or unified. Even full screen.

41
00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:37.553
History? The repository graph, with the agent’s branch highlighted.

42
00:02:37.593 --> 00:02:39.713
Click a commit, and there’s its diff.

43
00:02:40.433 --> 00:02:42.633
A worktree per agent? When it’s ready,

44
00:02:42.673 --> 00:02:46.353
one click merges its branch into main, as a merge or a squash.

45
00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.693
And the status bar follows your branch: pull, push and fetch, in one click.

46
00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:57.473
A file to tweak? The built-in editor opens on the project,

47
00:02:57.553 --> 00:02:59.073
or on an agent’s worktree.

48
00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:04.087
File tree, tabs, syntax highlighting, search, and modified lines,

49
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:05.207
marked in the margin.

50
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.040
Ctrl+S, and it’s saved. And if the agent changes your open file,

51
00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:11.320
the editor warns you.

52
00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:17.467
And now, the Kanban! To do, in progress, to review, done.

53
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:22.174
A ticket: a title, a description, acceptance criteria…

54
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.147
and a maximum number of loops.

55
00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:28.113
Right-click: edit, move to the top, delete.

56
00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:31.906
Start a ticket by hand… or switch on autopilot:

57
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.253
agents take tickets on their own, in order,

58
00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:36.813
up to the number of parallel agents you chose.

59
00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:42.573
Each ticket gets its own agent, its branch, its worktree with your .env files,

60
00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:44.893
and its own ports for tests.

61
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.627
And the agent loops until it gets there: every turn, it checks its criteria.

62
00:03:49.653 --> 00:03:51.360
Some missing? It goes again.

63
00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:56.707
On the card: the current loop, the criteria met, what’s already in place,

64
00:03:56.747 --> 00:03:58.467
and what the agent is doing right now.

65
00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:03.567
A blocked ticket frees its slot and lets you know. One click, and it goes again.

66
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:08.027
Its report shows as a card in the conversation. And in the sidebar,

67
00:04:08.107 --> 00:04:10.427
its card carries the ticket and its loop.

68
00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:16.247
Your workflow lives in the repo? Escouade draws it: every step, every agent,

69
00:04:16.287 --> 00:04:17.607
and the path taken.

70
00:04:19.033 --> 00:04:22.953
All criteria met? The ticket lands in To review, and you’re notified.

71
00:04:22.993 --> 00:04:27.193
Last loop without meeting them all? It goes there too, as “Partial goal”.

72
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.900
One click on Test: Escouade sets up the project,

73
00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.220
starts its servers on the ticket’s ports, waits for them to answer…

74
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.080
And opens your browser right on the feature!

75
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.280
The logs? In the Launch section, under the agent’s name.

76
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.920
And for any worktree agent, Prepare launch asks it how to test it.

77
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:52.473
Looks good? Approve! Escouade reruns the tests, commits with a generated message,

78
00:04:52.633 --> 00:04:54.793
merges into your branch and removes the worktree.

79
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.540
Rather a pull request, opened with gh? Or push the branch?

80
00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.340
It’s all set per project, down to conflicts and parallel agents.

81
00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:07.613
Something’s off? Send the ticket back to its agent, telling it what’s wrong.

82
00:05:07.693 --> 00:05:10.733
And if a test fails at approval, it goes back by itself.

83
00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:15.067
And finished tickets keep their outcome, their loops and their cost.

84
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Your tickets are in Jira, Trello or GitHub? Connect your account,

85
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.520
and link a source to your project.

86
00:05:21.833 --> 00:05:25.593
One click on Import: search, filter, tick.

87
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.233
And their acceptance criteria are detected on their own.

88
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:31.393
There they are in the Kanban, with their original key.

89
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.960
Escouade syncs their status, and comments on the ticket when it’s ready for review, then approved.

90
00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:41.607
Even better: label a ticket, and it lands in your Kanban on its own.

91
00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:48.333
Your project, in one click: set up its commands, the front end, the API, a worker,

92
00:05:48.373 --> 00:05:50.253
each with its own shell and folder.

93
00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:55.367
Run all, and everything starts: each command has its log and live status.

94
00:05:55.967 --> 00:05:59.327
One crashes? You know right away, with its exit code.

95
00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:07.227
A terminal? PowerShell, Git Bash or WSL, and zsh, bash and fish on Mac.

96
00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:09.587
With your shell’s autocomplete, history,

97
00:06:09.627 --> 00:06:11.387
and even your full-screen programs.

98
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.147
At the bottom, the status bar counts your active, waiting and finished agents.

99
00:06:17.173 --> 00:06:20.160
And it tracks your quotas: the 5-hour window, the week,

100
00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:21.600
and when they reset.

101
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.380
Today’s cost climbs live, with the memory and CPU Claude takes.

102
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Usage limit reached? The agent resumes by itself as soon as your quota is back.

103
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.560
And the stats: your input, cache and output tokens, your cost, by day,

104
00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.360
week or month, by project and by model.

105
00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.487
Stepping away? Turn on remote control:

106
00:06:43.567 --> 00:06:47.887
the agent’s session opens on claude.ai, and in the Claude app on your phone.

107
00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.920
What you send there shows up in Escouade too.

108
00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:56.453
Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, Copilot:

109
00:06:56.493 --> 00:06:58.733
each agent runs on the harness you pick.

110
00:06:59.033 --> 00:07:03.033
Harnesses, voice, Kanban, code hosts: everything is a plugin.

111
00:07:03.073 --> 00:07:05.113
And the store checks and signs them.

112
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.080
One click, and it’s installed.

113
00:07:08.533 --> 00:07:12.453
And more: settings for everything, down to stopping idle agents,

114
00:07:12.493 --> 00:07:16.213
a network proxy, keyboard shortcuts, and automatic updates.

115
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.000
Escouade runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.

116
00:07:21.333 --> 00:07:24.853
Escouade is free and open source. Download it at escouade.dev,

117
00:07:24.933 --> 00:07:27.093
and give your agents a squad!
